#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054503 :question/id #uuid "59d2e907-e788-5a22-93a9-c49ff94df6ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-190" :question/problem "Given ${x}^{2} + {y}^{2} = \\sin   ( y ) ,$ find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} \\\\ 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} - \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ ( 2 y - \\cos   ( y ) ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - \\frac{2 x}{2 y - \\cos   ( y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\frac{2 x}{\\cos   ( y ) - 2 y}\\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054504 :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054505 :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054572 :question/id #uuid "af328f52-53a1-5cc1-a542-6cad8eea4c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-6114" :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b801b5b5-e883-5963-9d61-ce1ae900a121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051609} {:answer/id #uuid "c59457b1-afd2-539c-8338-f8db720cb827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051610} {:answer/id #uuid "ea75b0c9-a987-5f4b-8eac-043dfcf76667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051611} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb9b6d7-f59f-5396-aa30-c9a89804c12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051612} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e3cda0-65ac-5d3f-9fdc-11c8ac57a8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb61154-9035-5b6a-ae9a-7ff0bd12aae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051608}] :question/id #uuid "c9ee4965-39c2-5212-af60-bf2b5270f94b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15134" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2}, 1)$ for the curve $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad705d6-c77f-5a47-9a1e-5798a2e69b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051615} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcedc71-cf7d-5b65-af9e-54428e26e058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051616} {:answer/id #uuid "e0009580-02f5-52dd-98f1-b07dbfc99cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051617} {:answer/id #uuid "33551d7d-90e1-5c07-b924-3ac69df5265e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{2}}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051618} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc5722-bf35-575a-8a09-a915ad74fff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "10c0ed9f-9e4a-5ad5-ae27-d4b346d556e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051614}] :question/id #uuid "53178a84-d201-5a6c-9c41-fb88e803ebbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-15140" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aab03da9e57863ce49011b6e6d9e1cd2cb64aa4c3eabfe5f323f505074c3b987.png)\n\nThe equation $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 6$ represents an ellipse. The normal line at the point $(\\sqrt{2}, 1)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae9c0a3-aa69-501e-9e6e-8e65972b3220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051633} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d87b76-34ca-5101-b173-4c4d2d6dd80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{14}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051634} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7a1216-d248-5790-8d4e-32c43f11b949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051635} {:answer/id #uuid "33ddcc32-b317-5d4d-8276-63aa3cc2da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051636} {:answer/id #uuid "696ae730-4f99-5f63-85b6-0d612888f8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051637}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5066968b-9998-5c60-9663-8898df06cb26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051632}] :question/id #uuid "3f326a76-bac1-526b-a118-b84853c87e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-15259" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a5fd15c2282e11fda4e98f64557d0b69d79bbcdc26946a3f494841233c680be.png)\n\nThe equation $3x^{2} - 2y^{2} = 4$ represents a hyperbola. The normal line at the point $(2, 2)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deda9bd5-0a70-5bc1-b365-653a4096b970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052178} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e286ab-82b6-521f-a0e3-519c6496b6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x^{2}}{y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052179} {:answer/id #uuid "0285dc00-9cd4-5f53-a20c-3394c0b05041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052180} {:answer/id #uuid "21281560-27ed-56ef-8e21-2c503d6346ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x^{2}}{3y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052181} {:answer/id #uuid "67977739-9f0d-5659-95d2-33541419e62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052182}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0bd529-6124-53d6-a57c-2bd1232e3ed3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052177}] :question/id #uuid "3ea4a517-4ea8-5b36-8f97-aa50b0bc7ec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-2282" :question/problem "Given that $x^{3} + y^{3} = 1$, calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af96d36e-687a-59e5-909b-f7a09761f434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\sin y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052184} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cbc9c6-297f-5f37-bbff-f5f24148453b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10 + 4x}{\\sin y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052185} {:answer/id #uuid "fbacdebd-7ce7-51ff-9cda-3fd5b936c1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(2x - 5))/(\\sin y + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052186} {:answer/id #uuid "27e213a0-d83b-5210-94f2-c741c94081b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10 - 4x}{\\cos y - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052187} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdbb430-36b3-5add-a676-b3d465f9a52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{-\\sin y - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052188}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0831952d-62ff-5473-8c5b-70ba3099ed21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052183}] :question/id #uuid "86a99e97-788a-5069-b4f7-bd85961ed56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2284" :question/problem "Given that $\\cos y + 2x^{2} - y = 10x$, calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69912a1-81cb-590a-a4f0-6a59692071f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x - y}{x + 2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052190} {:answer/id #uuid "e106e177-4f83-593d-bbfd-6b476fd27bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y - x}{x + 6y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052191} {:answer/id #uuid "12dc2652-f404-53ca-9e31-a37977a7e45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x - y}{x + 2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2acf29-3fe5-5a42-902b-b6bfa30b6933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x - y}{x - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052193} {:answer/id #uuid "7c70b813-19a1-5780-8b53-fbab347ea4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^{2} + y}{3x + 2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052194}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb8d303-b549-5306-9d82-21055554c63d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052189}] :question/id #uuid "2748254d-1dc6-5a6c-842a-c4422a28fcc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-2286" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $xy + y^{2} = 3x^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2546fc47-8c07-5115-814a-8146ced978bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288277} {:answer/id #uuid "57345f82-5b97-5af2-9839-7422717d4919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288637} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac550bb-b62f-5d41-baea-cefe6d3362bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289068} {:answer/id #uuid "a92bd509-6c75-5566-9d6e-1bebc673246a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289154} {:answer/id #uuid "d60b15ff-b4e3-5119-9aa2-888ab48baa08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2546fc47-8c07-5115-814a-8146ced978bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288277} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b645f0-053d-5f85-973d-6e01d88e18b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f5ae45c-0d29-593f-abd0-85c3a45aaf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-2287" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11eb4ca1-3088-577b-8f30-b5808544a999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330106} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab116bc-e40e-5d71-80b0-245bc4ee1484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330909} {:answer/id #uuid "7f492b9d-6afa-598a-9228-2c305e5229d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331073} {:answer/id #uuid "b3abbbd4-f00c-5565-9f4d-d1ae4e3cf803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331535} {:answer/id #uuid "f29131c4-16c3-54d0-8eb6-c764e2d48fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3abbbd4-f00c-5565-9f4d-d1ae4e3cf803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331535} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e69b9a7-aeba-5f73-aa1e-59bfa9d09e08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "421782a5-f8ee-574b-bd35-7a68ca94b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-53746" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ (2x+y)+(x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 0+1}{0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0+1}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233116 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3220f14-15f6-53f4-bf67-85844b3d99e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108505" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)=8{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}) \\\\ [({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}(y))] & =24{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =24{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y))}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233117 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "520ada25-cf40-56ac-b6dd-e3f2d526993e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108660" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}=8x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3} & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy]-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}) & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233118 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09438de3-169c-5930-a51b-e7ea865bc196" :question/math-academy-id "q-108681" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233500 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d412dae4-67ed-5206-9fdc-3a9133eaf377" :question/math-academy-id "q-14527" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(0)}{\\operatorname{cos}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b71c047d-d0a4-59a9-94d3-e9b03771936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410327} {:answer/id #uuid "cda0515f-1f4d-516f-be38-223dff8af225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410344} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a2a8e4-cff5-5de9-a017-294e1b3e3090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410347} {:answer/id #uuid "e659e298-eaa5-5dc6-b4eb-0351e4a2b803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410358} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d5b8d1-56ce-5658-ab8d-6cb521d7bf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d5b8d1-56ce-5658-ab8d-6cb521d7bf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410368} :answer-field/id #uuid "40bccfca-586d-50c1-9dd5-02226a8edd85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ada3b28b-b3a4-5dcc-bddd-f6c1a31f531f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14614" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $$\n\\displaystyle {m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233502 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c90af648-13de-501c-bf36-edfdcca21d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14697" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc45107eb076c0dc199718def7bb3a34b73ad61ca00bda61e09472a9ea589c84.png)\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$  represents a circle. The tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ on the circle intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-\\sqrt{2}) & =-1(x-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =-(0-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233645 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7217304d-4e39-5138-b636-a65ba43dac92" :question/math-academy-id "q-2288" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}=3,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)-2x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x)(y\\operatorname{ln}(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233647 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e3b5433-cb82-5a63-9431-f3be96f52398" :question/math-academy-id "q-2291" :question/problem "Given that ${y}^{2}-4x+y=2,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4x+y & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y & =2+4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2+4x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2y+1) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{2y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7452b504-26e3-597e-abfe-757af2492083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7452b504-26e3-597e-abfe-757af2492083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232853} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad98a1e-d9ad-53cc-aff5-9732df7a5c5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e593c2e1-263c-5294-9a7c-b566ea68ae19" :question/math-academy-id "q-341919" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(2,\\,7)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x+7}+\\sqrt{y+2}=6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x+7}+\\sqrt{y+2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+7)^{1/2}+(y+2)^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}(y+2)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(y+2)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+7)^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y+2}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+7}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\sqrt{x+7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\,7)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,7)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\sqrt{x+7}}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7+2}}{\\sqrt{2+7}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bcc75f9-63cb-52ca-bb8c-372d2be966bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bcc75f9-63cb-52ca-bb8c-372d2be966bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232855} :answer-field/id #uuid "35502148-dde5-5c8b-8e2d-a2a692207452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a60708d4-334c-5573-85a8-fee34ba1a832" :question/math-academy-id "q-341926" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,4)$ for the curve ${x}^{3}y=4x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x) \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4-3{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4-3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{4-3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}}\\right)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3(1)^{2}(4)}{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-8)}{1} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce48f81-46dd-5ebd-94aa-63f7a58d13d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce48f81-46dd-5ebd-94aa-63f7a58d13d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232877} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cbbfde7-1cf8-5750-b34b-e4f67915fe91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9f87893-d24b-5141-b10c-8c7c8da2c011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f87893-d24b-5141-b10c-8c7c8da2c011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232879} :answer-field/id #uuid "774a43a2-3fa6-5f99-9545-2002cf878d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4ca93831-6b46-56af-b1a8-6bb81dd5d559" :question/math-academy-id "q-342390" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $8y={x}^{2}+4x.$ The normal line at the point $(4,4)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(4,4).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y & ={x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4x) \\\\ 8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+4}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{x+2}{4}\\right)|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal line at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P,$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(10,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234142 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "44ff8394-c8e2-5860-8296-306905059970" :question/math-academy-id "q-53754" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=9.$ The normal line at the point $(3,\\,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,\\,3).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6x-4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-6x}{-4y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,\\,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{2y}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $$\n\\displaystyle {m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(3,\\,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(-3)+3 \\\\ y & =2+3 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234143 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96c94304-6fed-51f3-830a-46750ce98600" :question/math-academy-id "q-53755" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})=3{x}^{3}+10$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2x{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+10) \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})) & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\ 2(2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2})+(-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2} \\\\ 4xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}+(-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(4xy-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}{4xy-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcd17f-952e-5ec6-8fc1-e15b19233f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f524026-bb7b-5692-aa92-5df2f0fa376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-3y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242299} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fb17d5-81be-5f73-8b4b-2fbb56c7c484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242868} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e220dc-2da0-5e71-8e92-14bd79bb0955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243098} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff3c6a-3127-59cf-8abb-ff7552021965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e220dc-2da0-5e71-8e92-14bd79bb0955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243098} :answer-field/id #uuid "5685c02e-3a96-5d71-9a03-4ca4b0ff6731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "673feb27-4ae3-559c-903e-378e7e84b56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108367" :question/problem "Given that ${y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}=5,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)-4x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0384f4a9-12fe-5387-a1be-f88c7e01239b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241211} {:answer/id #uuid "129626b4-e403-5da1-8705-0a1f6a0c1510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241340} {:answer/id #uuid "81f2495e-8df6-56e4-90ed-bfeeda18086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242320} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bf0798-5176-5064-907f-27ffcf873486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf706940-1972-5b4d-8306-565d30014034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f2495e-8df6-56e4-90ed-bfeeda18086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242320} :answer-field/id #uuid "40437dbb-8029-5af3-8c3b-5299eddeaf71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee119daa-efcf-505f-b183-08b031d8df4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108475" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}=11,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+6x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(-6x)(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e436aa9-1f27-528c-9b27-85ed3f247f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}+1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241442} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e1e52-332d-5342-9c48-ba0f24733d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242671} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3b068a-44ff-5d2f-a0c9-b5478e80da18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{y}-1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242822} {:answer/id #uuid "d2124168-f20b-59e2-b795-fe1b9c41f12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}+2}{1+y{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243031} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8d9684-f50a-5be7-ae59-410c9f9af558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y{e}^{y}+1}{2+{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e1e52-332d-5342-9c48-ba0f24733d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242671} :answer-field/id #uuid "56334162-ef15-56a1-bf2b-a790116276b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7ce22a4d-4733-52a4-87c2-c2e0529b4bfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-108506" :question/problem "Given that  ${e}^{y}=2y-x,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y-x) \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}(2y)}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2-{e}^{y}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f63ca28-20f4-5ff8-9a12-4968c62840a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241602} {:answer/id #uuid "73dee891-e7dc-5aa4-b6c2-acb546cad109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{e}^{y}}+\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242206} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d54d64-2abf-5865-8588-d85adfb37e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{e}^{y}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242736} {:answer/id #uuid "c62afac1-7e4d-5490-bac9-5ce75731facc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}-\\frac{36}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242926} {:answer/id #uuid "e076c01e-595b-5ba6-9520-e692b16440c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{e}^{y}}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e076c01e-595b-5ba6-9520-e692b16440c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{e}^{y}}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46869f2-5fb3-5240-a863-435e22eea7b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f4c216f-b191-5d84-8924-850e05c75712" :question/math-academy-id "q-108509" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{3}{e}^{y}=9{x}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{3}{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{e}^{y} & =9{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{x}^{4}) \\\\ [({x}^{3})({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(3{x}^{2})({e}^{y})] & =36{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} & =36{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} & =36{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =36{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{36{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{36}{{e}^{y}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23441a47-fcaf-584c-b756-42a56d0bd7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241486} {:answer/id #uuid "46b49dea-0599-55e6-824f-cc37e7cb2889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241819} {:answer/id #uuid "785b91f5-856e-5c55-b803-5c29d2bf1a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242239} {:answer/id #uuid "e348121d-a49d-5158-a92a-50bc6e1aca3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243205} {:answer/id #uuid "f501c58f-0ae2-5d66-8c6f-a48ea346d0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "785b91f5-856e-5c55-b803-5c29d2bf1a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242239} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfc33ef-1926-5400-9715-26b6e909a1fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56e6a335-1c6d-5f20-b447-7c7858535bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108640" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n Note that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)]+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38abdefd-3252-574c-ad83-da30c6b82e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}}{7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241685} {:answer/id #uuid "bb545dae-a3c6-5bfb-9023-948c37103acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242823} {:answer/id #uuid "c5136acb-020b-505c-a4e7-1cfbfb2cc0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242917} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcd5ecf-e29d-52a6-8e4f-06c8bd373504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242992} {:answer/id #uuid "d32818a8-5eed-5b07-a7ac-495f052b994d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5136acb-020b-505c-a4e7-1cfbfb2cc0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242917} :answer-field/id #uuid "4934faa7-2c01-504f-90c9-b63344c5db8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82a0f4a1-b7fe-533e-9033-fcc9236329fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-108655" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}=12{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2} & =12{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ [21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x \\\\ 21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+6y) & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ce074c-f5ee-5790-955f-744688452a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241807} {:answer/id #uuid "7e670689-9386-512c-80a1-9ffb0405857d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242290} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fe8801-fc93-5cb1-b234-3374bb79395a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242633} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b24f33-d38a-5c21-a5c3-2817021cafc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243063} {:answer/id #uuid "de835654-3bb0-5b6e-95d2-3def6a996d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ce074c-f5ee-5790-955f-744688452a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241807} :answer-field/id #uuid "504aa2d6-12d2-5a6b-8911-82cd37da7948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffb47def-243e-5613-87da-6b6ae8fdf163" :question/math-academy-id "q-108674" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\right)}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e607f78-385d-53f6-ab41-74ac877a5a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241591} {:answer/id #uuid "32897127-ad14-5eef-9a81-301deb3e2a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241624} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a655d-56b1-5123-a490-d7bf85825ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243025} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7ba673-b558-5e38-9cf1-92dadf966d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd113b97-7abf-5d30-9cb0-076358176a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32897127-ad14-5eef-9a81-301deb3e2a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241624} :answer-field/id #uuid "52dfb695-349e-5936-9fe3-1ad1eeda4511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "85cec444-2f0a-598c-bff4-89c6e3584a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-108679" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-{\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0631f001-9df9-51cc-90ca-207a4ec9d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241234} {:answer/id #uuid "07898e0a-df78-56eb-948b-d6624803c46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241242} {:answer/id #uuid "514bec40-43cf-5921-b0f1-a5f625af7922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241907} {:answer/id #uuid "dd352c42-de2c-5892-a3e6-2f07f36b728e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243149} {:answer/id #uuid "fefc7f0b-937f-5705-8e05-726d03e28905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0631f001-9df9-51cc-90ca-207a4ec9d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241234} :answer-field/id #uuid "505d2914-6e4a-5059-95e6-b60f71fd8484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4792eb7-cef8-5807-bfe2-869a28aaef03" :question/math-academy-id "q-109152" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ceb279-b498-5112-be99-e2c7cd035a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272547} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e4790-ea4f-51e5-bc2d-de3c1f027829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272776} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8c06dc-25a2-5876-97fd-28d301745799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272791} {:answer/id #uuid "301acec7-8427-545f-ab2b-e5f848335317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272815} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f6a3c-5880-5001-b1f4-a6e54abf9363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e4790-ea4f-51e5-bc2d-de3c1f027829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272776} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce63663-348f-55d2-838d-fea135c9c3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20636dab-8594-514a-8d86-b7740f06ab8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14680" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}=8.$ The tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1).$  \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4x+12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{12y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{x}{3y}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1)+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0003a375-681f-5467-ad7f-0e316d8ea2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272387} {:answer/id #uuid "05778dc0-da5c-5576-9b3b-199472c2e96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272438} {:answer/id #uuid "5734619a-c992-5673-8fa2-ebec216e0f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273134} {:answer/id #uuid "c50030c3-6a0a-5a7c-ac24-626a9fd6fbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274095} {:answer/id #uuid "e0211d9e-5309-54cf-b139-6378966bcd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05778dc0-da5c-5576-9b3b-199472c2e96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272438} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f62dc2-04ac-5e7f-82e4-2247fc1fb5bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff4af0d3-ebf7-53d5-b132-2755c7e5429b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15130" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$ The normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x}{-6y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{3y}\\right)}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =1(x+3) \\\\ y & =(x+3)+1 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\ 0 & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-4,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} {:answer/id #uuid "793b6c32-8350-533d-bf9d-724d40064c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{y}}{6x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288865} {:answer/id #uuid "a3589f85-19fc-5bd4-ac3b-96bf622c2422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289122} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d85bbf-5174-563d-a666-9a94bd4cf6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289532} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c90f5-ce7c-52d7-8488-fe25083eb2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f58fa9b-a3f6-53aa-a4c8-4680d5988ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "623f14ca-5670-5819-81c3-5ed253df18c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2289" :question/problem "Given that  $y-{2}^{y}=3{x}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y-{2}^{y} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-{2}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){2}^{y})\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =6x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078c6f54-8a90-570d-b35c-a74273909664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{3y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288093} {:answer/id #uuid "2246442e-020d-574a-8441-1430c141e7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{cot}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288251} {:answer/id #uuid "a389ed33-85ab-5efc-b38c-37f637636ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289125} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1f7f5-2930-5b7c-b842-100eb308f5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289219} {:answer/id #uuid "cb43772c-130c-57f1-8c10-6233e01d16ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1f7f5-2930-5b7c-b842-100eb308f5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289219} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e55e3cc-8566-5ead-b496-998e3c5f2250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "610d7896-a073-528d-972b-ac6b8950750a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2290" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2}=y,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\ 3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)-2y\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2y+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd0cb33b-8767-54e7-a818-0bec60f8663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd0cb33b-8767-54e7-a818-0bec60f8663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341} :answer-field/id #uuid "71661f85-5585-5ca0-ad3a-dc9dadf683ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c578341-fd5b-5ea9-b094-029b8faa3d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-312258" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f6f736-4009-5b4f-8058-a10414188390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f6f736-4009-5b4f-8058-a10414188390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315353} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e5c64b-d713-5876-b513-033b01dba8b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a97764f-f130-5df7-bef4-c5372f6f59c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-341915" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae7cae9-79ff-5f39-85da-df94153947dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae7cae9-79ff-5f39-85da-df94153947dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315719} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd4eb5e-7bd4-5910-bd71-b6d0c5d615da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "be11712d-8184-5176-b182-3e96ce7e8e3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-341918" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}-{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d997c12d-a03b-5296-a830-6db4be672df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d997c12d-a03b-5296-a830-6db4be672df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316331} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9fad05-4c08-5c4b-bf58-5859bb52824f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45bf2a7e-b636-5c97-90c2-25563ca38da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342311" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ for the curve $2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+[2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}]-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\right)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-2(1)(1)^{2}}{6(1)^{2}+2(1)^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c48093-236c-529a-a8a5-bc47c821574a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c48093-236c-529a-a8a5-bc47c821574a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315450} :answer-field/id #uuid "0833a293-5ce1-5cef-ab4d-f3003c06003a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316569} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d876ff73-6c82-5b9c-ae49-36253a7b0032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d876ff73-6c82-5b9c-ae49-36253a7b0032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316325} :answer-field/id #uuid "92c6806c-bbda-5418-b219-934b1a0e61f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9e44cd61-adda-5320-9484-a3532ad9dce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342321" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+xy=6{y}^{2}.$ The normal line at the point $(2,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27bf30c6-e7c4-5ec3-b883-0faeb6a3eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27bf30c6-e7c4-5ec3-b883-0faeb6a3eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315343} :answer-field/id #uuid "552e72d4-92af-586a-ab5f-e83510ccaf06" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316744} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f248050-bc2a-5707-92f0-c9a6e0f9c621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f248050-bc2a-5707-92f0-c9a6e0f9c621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315920} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e5fed4-d7ce-583b-afc4-57ba2adaf642" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb9cd239-2bce-5b80-bfae-5a75f2ef2946" :question/math-academy-id "q-342325" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ The tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,2).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ 8x+18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{8x}{18y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{9y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,2)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{4x}{9y}\\right)|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 0}{9\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =0(x-0) \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(0,\\,2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61519a5-48e2-5b80-93a3-3327fea9fa62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61519a5-48e2-5b80-93a3-3327fea9fa62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316151} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a97fa83-0244-5f79-8c88-ebc2c70b353e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2215a7c8-f9b0-5b37-8a62-2faf1da168c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2215a7c8-f9b0-5b37-8a62-2faf1da168c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67bea70-6a8d-5d6f-bd75-2c9222038429" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69007ed8-dcbc-586e-9203-0e7716f8329c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342387" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+2y=20.$ The tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,8).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2y & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ 2x+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & ={(-x)|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-2x+12 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(6,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5c20d41-435d-55d0-a172-c27f052bf5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5c20d41-435d-55d0-a172-c27f052bf5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316491} :answer-field/id #uuid "10d2e3ee-4d35-53d9-b4e2-090c4fa16f8a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e056870-a5ab-500f-9df4-56cffb9cced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e056870-a5ab-500f-9df4-56cffb9cced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315292} :answer-field/id #uuid "48675c26-937f-5a4a-9678-5bc409152df1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "019eae01-b09f-5893-aaf7-0f3dd1c87dc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342401" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c89b1d28ce7692f54e0fbf32c1d99b091e24729c837883523d6150450decb17.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=20x$ represents an ellipse. The tangent line at the point $(4,4)$ on the ellipse intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{20-8x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10-4x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{10-4x}{y}\\right)|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-4\\cdot 4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c91537-c8bb-5ae8-9883-50855ca49ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c91537-c8bb-5ae8-9883-50855ca49ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315501} :answer-field/id #uuid "6989c637-7d71-59ab-9be9-dd8aee348fa6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6fd9cb-d9e4-58c5-a9db-de33ef15e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6fd9cb-d9e4-58c5-a9db-de33ef15e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315173} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bb43d8-6e16-595b-a856-77cccca933a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96578920-5978-5ccd-b815-b15c23dd1fbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-342526" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d997ec4e099fc8be5b34a51bdfd0e9511915c348671d9832ed640f0ec4360602.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=15y$ represents an ellipse. The normal line at the point $(3,3)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,3).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =15y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(15y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(15y) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =15\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-15\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ (2y-15)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{8x}{2y-15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{8x}{2y-15}\\right)|}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal line at the point $(3,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{3}{8}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{9}{8}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}x & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ x & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19984c41-2e9b-55a7-a17c-f6a14e8face7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330171} {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} {:answer/id #uuid "90067c4f-ae37-5430-8ae1-5960ae9a9184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331230} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc8021-894c-56c3-9e32-003542b0ae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0b2fc2-d00a-5cb2-8558-20bf23e0f5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8576bd1-557c-5066-8b28-c3980d1e6e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "178ea495-c20f-5cdc-a1d6-8971b012cd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-53745" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab9861f-bd24-5e05-a3df-1a270b940998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330329} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4f63f-b107-58fc-9a69-479fc30539ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331129} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a74c7f-fa05-586c-91b2-b13729730ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331534} {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} {:answer/id #uuid "f3011a9e-cdfd-5574-baad-72ca2f4d675d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} :answer-field/id #uuid "8801c2b3-5109-5311-a205-3b2d57c10695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c73f48a1-5f73-5fca-87a5-5adf2b36d0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-53747" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b557f6-3edb-5181-83a9-d501c8f38e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330576} {:answer/id #uuid "4615b002-6927-5b19-8fec-0a981bf28557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330580} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} {:answer/id #uuid "ab557b79-efa8-5221-881f-bc531ab4bbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331467} {:answer/id #uuid "eae5ddb8-9bf5-5595-a494-19c67808b88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c83ec8-c729-560d-934b-2c4ab7bca24f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b7bca4e-70fe-57a8-8703-2bc1be5d133a" :question/math-academy-id "q-53750" :question/problem "![](images/fa/faa8a8e0521972fa860d474ac079405b6930df6c47fda05808fdf242d6d6922b.png)\n\nThe equation $xy=5$ represents a hyperbola. The tangent line at the point $(1,5)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-5) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y & =-5(x-1)+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-5\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0fc10a-2cbd-5633-bb2f-ea7aaaa841c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336123} {:answer/id #uuid "16292cbd-8350-5bd3-96d9-795ef552cf0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240} {:answer/id #uuid "1e57b365-540c-5e8a-8331-f6790e70b06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+2{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336311} {:answer/id #uuid "23a544b4-0d38-57bb-983c-e904eb2a25c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336367} {:answer/id #uuid "ee47ac7c-c346-5761-8902-7e42ed6ca55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16292cbd-8350-5bd3-96d9-795ef552cf0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6bf4581-0ffc-537f-82d9-87405f04b109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "454b7cf5-7862-57c7-a910-8dbc0dac1fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72213" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)]+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}) & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5fbf3c-f1c3-5c9f-b557-87e97eea1f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336440} {:answer/id #uuid "503533c1-419f-5912-992d-a844c7898071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336766} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcbe671-145d-5fc4-ac7c-f87de3e487a0" :answer/type {:db/